# Handling Multiple WebSockets

In the last reading, one client and one server exchanged messages. The interesting version is **many clients**, with the server passing messages between them. That is a chat room, and it is what this reading builds: the same shape of server as before, a page worth typing into, and one loop added on the server.

Remember the arrangement from two readings ago: clients never talk to each other directly. Every message goes from a client to the server, and the server sends it onward.

## The Chat Page

The demo is `ws-demo/chat-server.js` and its page in `ws-demo/chat-page/`, next to this notebook. The video builds the page first:

```{walkthrough}
title: The chat page
step: The markup
file: ws-demo/chat-page/index.html
region: markup
A minimalist chat: a list of messages, and a form for sending one.
@"ul id=": Empty on purpose. Every message this list ever shows will arrive over the WebSocket.
@"placeholder": Where a message is typed.
@"<button": Submits the form, which also makes the Enter key send from inside the input, the way people expect chat to work.

step: The connection and the references
file: ws-demo/chat-page/index.html
region: references
The script opens with the familiar connection lines, then grabs the three elements it will need.
@"new WebSocket(": The same connection line as the last two readings: connect back to wherever the page came from, over `wss:` if the page itself arrived over `https:`.
@"binaryType": Also from the last reading: ask for binary messages in a form that decodes back to text.
@"querySelector('#input')": The input to read messages out of, the list to add them to, and the form to listen to.

step: Sending on submit
file: ws-demo/chat-page/index.html
region: outgoing
@"addEventListener('submit'": Runs when the button is clicked or Enter is pressed. The listener's argument is the submit event.
@"preventDefault": A form's default submit behavior is to navigate to a new page. That would throw away this page and the open WebSocket with it, so the first thing the listener does is turn the default off.
@"ws.send(messageEl.value)": Whatever was typed goes to the server. Notice what does not happen here: nothing is added to the list on screen.
@"messageEl.value = ''": Clear the input so the next message can be typed straight away.
```

## The Broadcast

Now the server. A message arrives on *one* socket and has to reach *all* of them. The `ws` library keeps the set of connections for us, as **`wss.clients`**:

```{walkthrough}
title: The broadcast, and what comes back
step: Forwarding to every client
file: ws-demo/chat-server.js
region: broadcast
This is the whole difference between the echo server and a chat room.
@"addEventListener('message'": The same message listener as the last reading. What changed is what the callback does with the message.
@"for (const client of wss.clients)": Not `socket.send`. The message is forwarded to every connected client, and `wss.clients` is the server's own, always-current set of them.
@"readyState": The state of one connection. A socket in the set may still be mid-handshake, or may have closed a moment ago; only `WebSocket.OPEN` can receive, so every other state is skipped.
@"data.toString()": Makes certain what goes out is a string, whichever form it arrived in.

step: Showing what comes back
file: ws-demo/chat-page/index.html
region: incoming
Back on the page, the client's message listener, which hears every message in the room, including the ones this same page sent.
@"typeof messageEvent.data": Reads the payload whether it arrived as text or as binary, the pattern from the last reading.
@"createElement": DOM work from Course 2: build a new `li`, fill it, and append it.
@"innerText": The message text becomes the list item's text.
@"messages.append": The video's first version of this listener was a `console.log(data)`; once the plumbing worked, it was upgraded to put messages on the page.
```

## Why the Loop Looks the Way It Does

Three decisions in those few server lines, each deliberate.

**Replying on `socket` would have been wrong.** Inside the `message` listener, `socket` is the one client who sent the message. `socket.send(...)` answers the sender, which is what the echo server did, and an echo is not a chat room. Broadcasting means going through `wss.clients`, the whole set.

**`client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN` is the safety check.** A connection has a lifecycle: `CONNECTING`, then `OPEN`, then `CLOSING` and `CLOSED`. Only an open socket can receive, and sending to one in any other state is an error. The set can genuinely contain the others, such as a client mid-handshake, or one that vanished a moment ago when somebody shut a laptop.

**The sender is included.** They sit in `wss.clients` like everyone else, so their own message comes back to them from the server. That is deliberate: every message on every screen arrived the same way, through the server, and no client special-cases its own.

That last point answers a fair objection. For a single browser, none of this looks necessary: the submit handler could have appended the message straight to the list, no server involved, and the page would seem to work. It would also be a private notepad. What makes it a chat room is that everything you see took the round trip through the server, because that is the same trip everyone else's messages take.

## The Broadcast, Running

The loop and its safety check can run right here, with stand-ins for the sockets the `ws` library would hand us:

```{node autorun}
// Stand-ins for the sockets the ws library would hand us.
const OPEN = 1;
const CONNECTING = 0;
const CLOSED = 3;

function makeClient(name, readyState) {
  const client = { name: name, readyState: readyState, received: [] };

  client.send = function (message) {
    client.received.push(message);
  };

  return client;
}

const clients = [
  makeClient('ada', OPEN),
  makeClient('grace', OPEN),
  makeClient('lin', CONNECTING),
  makeClient('alan', CLOSED)
];

function broadcast(message) {
  for (const client of clients) {
    if (client.readyState === OPEN) {
      client.send(message);
    }
  }
}

broadcast('hello from browser 1');

for (const client of clients) {
  console.log(client.name + ' received ' + client.received.length + ' message(s)');
}
```

Two of the four got the message. The one still connecting and the one already gone were skipped, which is exactly what the `readyState` check is for. Try changing `lin`'s state to `OPEN` and running the cell again.

## Run It Yourself

This is the demo to actually run, because a chat room only exists between windows. Start it (run `npm install` inside `ws-demo` first if you have not):

```
$ cd "ws-demo"
$ node chat-server.js
Listening on port 3000
```

Open `http://localhost:3000` in **two** browser windows side by side, and type a message into one:

1. It appears in both windows at the same moment. It shows up in the sender's own window because the server sent it back, not because the page added it locally.
2. Open a third window and send from there; all three show it. The video's browsers happened to be on one computer, but nothing in the code knows that, and remote machines would behave identically.
3. In the Network tab of any window, the `101` request's **Messages** pane shows the same broadcasts arriving.
4. Close one window and keep chatting in the others. The `readyState` check is what lets the loop walk past a connection that has gone away.

Stop the server with `Ctrl+C` when you are done.

## Exercises

1. Complete `broadcast` so every open client receives the message and no closed or connecting one does.

    ```{node}
    const OPEN = 1;
    const CONNECTING = 0;
    const CLOSED = 3;

    function makeClient(name, readyState) {
      const client = { name: name, readyState: readyState, received: [] };

      client.send = function (message) {
        client.received.push(message);
      };

      return client;
    }

    const clients = [
      makeClient('ada', OPEN),
      makeClient('grace', OPEN),
      makeClient('lin', CONNECTING),
      makeClient('alan', CLOSED)
    ];

    function broadcast(rawData) {
      // TODO: send rawData, as a string, to every client whose readyState is OPEN
    }

    broadcast('hello everyone');

    console.log(clients[0].received, clients[3].received);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const OPEN = 1;
    const CONNECTING = 0;
    const CLOSED = 3;

    function makeClient(name, readyState) {
      const client = { name: name, readyState: readyState, received: [] };

      client.send = function (message) {
        client.received.push(message);
      };

      return client;
    }

    const clients = [
      makeClient('ada', OPEN),
      makeClient('grace', OPEN),
      makeClient('lin', CONNECTING),
      makeClient('alan', CLOSED)
    ];

    function broadcast(rawData) {
      for (const client of clients) {
        if (client.readyState === OPEN) {
          client.send(rawData.toString());
        }
      }
    }

    broadcast('hello everyone');

    console.log(clients[0].received, clients[3].received);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(clients[0].received[0]).should('equal', 'hello everyone').run('The first open client should receive the message', 'Reached the first open client');
    wrap(clients[1].received[0]).should('equal', 'hello everyone').run('Every open client should receive it, not only the first. Loop over all of them', 'Reached every open client');
    wrap(clients[2].received.length).should('equal', 0).run('A client that is still connecting should be skipped. Compare its `readyState` with `OPEN`', 'Skipped the connecting client');
    wrap(clients[3].received.length).should('equal', 0).run('A client that has closed should be skipped too, since sending to it is an error', 'Skipped the closed client');
    ```

2. Complete the submit handler so sending a message does not reload the page, and the input is cleared afterwards.

    ```{node}
    // Stand-ins for the page and the socket.
    let prevented = false;
    const submitEvent = { preventDefault: function () { prevented = true; } };

    const messageEl = { value: 'hello everyone' };

    const sent = [];
    const ws = { send: function (data) { sent.push(data); } };

    function handleSubmit(submitEvent) {
      // TODO: stop the form from navigating, send the input's value
      // over the socket, then clear the input
    }

    handleSubmit(submitEvent);

    console.log(prevented, sent, messageEl.value);
    ```
    ```+solution
    // Stand-ins for the page and the socket.
    let prevented = false;
    const submitEvent = { preventDefault: function () { prevented = true; } };

    const messageEl = { value: 'hello everyone' };

    const sent = [];
    const ws = { send: function (data) { sent.push(data); } };

    function handleSubmit(submitEvent) {
      submitEvent.preventDefault();

      ws.send(messageEl.value);

      messageEl.value = '';
    }

    handleSubmit(submitEvent);

    console.log(prevented, sent, messageEl.value);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(prevented).should('equal', true).run('Call `submitEvent.preventDefault()`, or submitting the form navigates away and takes the WebSocket with it', 'Prevented the default submit');
    wrap(sent[0]).should('equal', 'hello everyone').run('Send the input\'s `value` over the socket', 'Sent the message');
    wrap(messageEl.value).should('equal', '').run('Clear the input afterwards by setting its `value` to an empty string', 'Cleared the input');
    wrap(sent.length).should('equal', 1).run('Send exactly one message per submit', 'Sent it once');
    ```

3. An echo where a chat should be

    ```{mcq}
    A first attempt at the chat server answers each message like this, and testers report that everyone sees only their own messages.

    ~~~js
    socket.addEventListener('message', (messageEvent) => {
      socket.send(messageEvent.data);
    });
    ~~~

    What is the fix?
    correct: Correct! `socket` is only the sender, so this code answers the one person who already had the message. Broadcasting means looping over `wss.clients`, the server's set of every connection, and sending to each open one.
    wrong: The message does reach the server, and it does come back. Ask who `socket` refers to inside this listener, and who never hears anything.
    [x] Loop over `wss.clients` and send to every open connection, instead of only to `socket`
    [ ] Call `socket.send` once per connected user, passing each user's name as a second argument
    [ ] Replace `addEventListener` with `on`, which delivers the reply to every client
    [ ] Have each client forward the messages it receives on to the other clients
    ```

4. The missing safety check

    ```{mcq}
    A version of the broadcast leaves out one line, and now the server can crash when someone closes a window at the wrong moment and another message is sent.

    ~~~js
    for (const client of wss.clients) {
      client.send(data.toString());
    }
    ~~~

    Which line is missing?
    correct: Correct! `wss.clients` can contain connections that are still opening or have already closed, and sending to either is an error. Checking `client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN` skips them.
    wrong: Every entry in `wss.clients` really is a connection, but not every connection is in a state that can receive. Which line checked for that?
    [x] `if (client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN)` around the send
    [ ] `submitEvent.preventDefault()` before the loop
    [ ] `const data = messageEvent.data` above the loop
    [ ] `client.send(data)` without the `toString()`
    ```

